# Findings gitleaks has reported that are not secrets.
#
# One line per finding, as `<commit>:<path>:<rule>:<line>`. Every entry needs a
# reason above it. An ignore file nobody can audit is worse than a scanner that
# occasionally cries wolf — if you cannot explain why a line is here, the right
# move is to remove it and change the code instead.

# A synthetic FIRMS credential in a test — thirty-two hex characters counting
# from zero, which is not quoted here because the scanner reads this file too
# and reported the quotation as a second leak.
#
# The test it belongs to is the one PROVING the real key never reaches an error
# message, since FIRMS puts its credential in the URL path and a failed fetch
# quotes the URL into an error that is persisted as source health.
#
# The literal is gone from the working tree — it is a named constant now, and
# deliberately not key-shaped. This entry covers the commit that already carries
# it. Note the commit hash: rewriting this branch's history changes it, and a
# fingerprint pointing at a commit that no longer exists silently stops
# suppressing anything.
